    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d823">

                <pb facs="1026" n="916" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg916"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.003/775: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-09-24T12:00:00-04:00">September 24,
                        1931—noon</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>337. Tour 614, September 21, noon, paragraph 1.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d823-f1"
                    /></p>
                <p>In reply to Doctor Wang’s letter you should inform him that this Government had
                    actually instructed you to be prepared at once to go to Nanking, but that in the
                    light of the events of the past 5 days the conclusion has been reached that you
                    should remain for the present where you are and you have been so instructed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d823-f1" n="4" xml:id="d823-f1-content">
                <p><ref target="frus1931-41v01#pg_2"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        Japan, 1931–1941, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, p. 2</ref>. Par.
                    1 refers to Dr. Wang’s letter quoted in telegram No. 592, September 17, 1931,
                    from the Minister in China, <ref target="#pg914">p. 914</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
